  "summary": "Four preprints apply single-cell genetics, eDNA, in vitro and metagenomic approaches to snow algae, diatoms, corals and plankton.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of molecular and genomic data to infer ecological traits of marine and polar organisms. Single-cell population genetics of Chlainomonas sp. blooms indicates a haploid-diploid life cycle with clonal reproduction and likely high selfing. In two Southern Ocean diatoms, in vitro work distinguishes proton-pumping rhodopsin light-harvesting strategies under varying iron and light spectra. Seawater eDNA metabarcoding at Mermaid Reef tracks coral community restructuring after a 2025 heatwave and matches photo-transect cover and assemblage patterns. A comparison of global open-ocean plankton diversity across occurrence, abundance, biomass and metagenomic data reports a latitudinal gradient. What is new is the explicit cross-checking of molecular readouts against conventional census methods, most clearly in the coral and plankton studies.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-27"
}
